  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea of this approach is to use a mean-field variational autoencoder (VAE) to learn a latent representation of optimal step functions, conditioned on constraints that enforce the desired properties (binary step function with exactly half 1's). This autoencoder architecture will map high-resolution step functions to low-dimensional latent variables, capturing functional similarities. The optimization will use projected gradient descent in the latent space, combined with multi-resolution expansion, to iteratively improve solutions. \n\nThe key differentiators from previous methods are:\n- Problem reformulation as a latent variable variational autoencoder with feature matching loss\n- Two-stage training: supervised denoising autoencoder + unsupervised guided sampling\n- Exploration-exploitation balance through latent space expansion\n- Multi-resolution constraint propagation through weighted interpolation\n- Probabilistic binary rounding to handle gradient descent in latent space\n\nThe proposed approach specifically targets the bottleneck of local optima and limited search space by leveraging structure learning and mean-field approximations. It assumes that optimal solutions can be represented by approximately sinusoidal patterns with controlled correlation, making latent space exploration feasible.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- Latent vector z \u2208 \u211d\u1d39\n- Encoder network E: (\u211d\u207f \u2192 \u211d\u1d39)\n- Decoder network D: (\u211d\u1d39 \u2192 \u211d\u207f)\n\nObjective functions:\n- Reconstruction Lrec(h||h_lab): Mean squared error between generated h (D(z)) and labeled h\n- KL divergence Lkl: Mean-field approximation between reconstructed and standard normally distributed latent variables\n- Regularization constraints for optimization:\n  Let hstructured = D(E(h)): Structural constraints are enforced via latent space gradient projection\n\nOptimization formulation:\nmin\u2097\u2098\u2099 max\u2096 (1/dx) \u222b hx(1-h(x+k))dx\nSubject to:\n 0 \u2264 hi \u2264 1\n \u222b0\u00b2h(x)dx = 1\n h(x) is binary\n\nThe mean-field VAE effectively relaxes the optimization by:\n- Using latent space gradients to enforce solution constraints\n- Incorporating weighted regularization terms for alignment with known optimal patterns\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize Gaussian latent space and associated networks (encoder, decoder, critic):\n\nStep 1: Latent network initialization \n- Convolutional encoder with progressively downsampling layers\n- Fully connected decoder mirrored from encoder\n- Gaussian mixture prior in latent space\n- Adam optimizer with fixed weight decay\n\nStep 2: Supervised denoising training\n- Pair inputs with random corruptions for encoder-decoder\n- Train autoencoder with reconstruction+KL divergence loss\n- Apply label-conditioned dataset augmentation\n\nStep 3: Entropy-guided sampling\n- Compute free energy in latent space (ELBO)\n- Select points with gradient magnitude matching top entropy bands\n- Use generative model to progressively expand solutions\n\nStep 4: Multi-scale reconstruction\n- Define pyramid of resolutions: 384 \u2192 512 \u2192 768 \u2192 1024\n- Use interpolation weight functions to propagate features\n- Apply entropy band selection based on energy density\n\nStep 5: Optimization loop\n- Latent space projected gradient descent using silhouette constraint checks\n- Compute collision metrics between candidate and losing boundaries\n- Apply binary rounding during gradient projection\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Latent space dimensions: \n  M=32 (base) \u2192 M=48 (intermediate) \u2192 M=128 (full)\n- KL weighting: 1.0 \u2192 0.5 \u2192 0.2 during structural learning\n- Sample selection: entropy-weighted (50\u00d7) Conditioned by label quality\n- Hierarchical training:\n  First: single period sinusoidal forced oscillations\n  Second: harmonic mean regularizer\n- Resolution configuration:\n  Lower stages (n\u2264256): Greedy expansion\n  Upper stages (n\u2265512): PCA-constrained\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n-Peak time usage: ~100 seconds for resolution progression\n- Budget allocation:\n  Autoencoder training: 300 seconds\n  Constraint propagation: 700 seconds\n  Optimization runs: 300 seconds\n-Early termination triggers based on:\n  Plateau detection (running mean improvement)\n  Consistency failures\n  Windowed constraint checks\n\nImplementation includes:\n-Self-correcting interpolation modules\n-Elastic development monitoring\n-Parallelization aware scheduling\n-Fallback projection mechanisms\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFinal validation includes:\n-Exact integral verification (n/2 ones)\n-Structural watermarks (correlation profile matching)\n-Guidance projection successes\n-Backup expansion paths (lag failure)\n\nReturn structure:\n(h_final, c5_bound, resolution)\nwhere c5_bound = (2.0/n) * max_k (np.correlate(h_final, 1-h_final, mode='full').max)\n\nThe algorithm ensures statistical robustness through latent function expansion and embeds self-monitoring for entropy and gradient alignment.",
